{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 22, :comments ["<b>And HaShem said, etc. — Behold the man, etc.</b> One must raise the question: why was El not concerned to command the man concerning the tree of life, lest he forestall and eat from it and live forever before eating from the tree of knowledge?" "<b>Further,</b> the verse says <b>הָיָה</b> (was) — whereas it should have said ״הוּא״ (he is). Further, it should have said only ״הֵן הָאָדָם כְּאֶחָד מִמֶּנּוּ״. Further, how can it be justified to compare him to elohim on the side of knowing good and evil? Further, how can it be justified to say the word ״מִמֶּנּוּ״ — heaven forbid that it suggests there is above a likeness [to Him], and it should have said ״כְּאֶחָד מִכֶּם״ or ״כֵּאלֹהִים״?" "<b>However,</b> since HaShem had already preceded and commanded the man concerning the tree of knowledge, HaShem was not concerned about the man eating from the tree of life — whether from his own side or from the side of the tempter. From his own side — because he was devoid of comprehension and awareness to devise schemes to ascend to the heights of physical achievements; from the side of the tempter — because it is the tempter's nature not to entice a man regarding something in which he has benefit and merit, and it is obvious that he would not entice him to eat from the tree of life. And if HaShem had commanded him concerning the tree of life along with the tree of knowledge, the tempter would have provoked him to eat from the tree of life for the sake of nullifying the commandment, and it is possible that he would have found an opening to entice even the man himself. Therefore HaShem was wise not to command him concerning this, for in this way the tempter would not provoke him; and once he ate from the tree of knowledge and he is in the awareness of something fitting for him, there enters the category of doubt — perhaps he will stretch out his hand to eat and live forever." "And for this concern HaShem spoke a word of wisdom, and this is the measure of His words: ״הֵן הָאָדָם הָיָה כְּאַחַד״ — meaning that HaShem created him as one, meaning as the oneness of the One acknowledged in the world, which is His oneness, blessed be He, as it says ״נַעֲשֶׂה אָדָם בְּצַלְמֵנוּ כִּדְמוּתֵנוּ״, and He granted him dominion over the lower realms, as it says ״וְיִרְדּוּ״ etc., and this action proceeded <b>מִמֶּנּוּ</b> — meaning, from the man — to know good and evil, for I had commanded him not to eat from the tree of knowledge of good and evil; but he sinned and attained the knowledge, and since he attained the knowledge there is reason to fear that through the medium of the knowledge he will choose to eat from the tree of life and live forever. And I found proof for the interpretation of ״מִמֶּנּוּ״ from the words of Onkelos, who translated ״מִמֶּנּוּ״ — ״מִנֵּיהּ״ (from him)." "<b>Further,</b> the matter may be explained in this manner: HaShem, on the day He commanded him concerning the tree of knowledge, commanded two things: the first, not to eat from the tree of knowledge; and the second, if he does eat he is subject to the remedy of death, which is His saying ״כִּי בְּיוֹם״ etc. ״תָּמוּת״ — for this is the rectification of the matter, and it was like a לָאו (negative commandment) whose עֲשֵׂה (positive commandment) is its rectification. And we have already written above that but for the sin, the man would have attained the ascent to be sustained in the eternal world in the form that he was, and he would have dwelling in the upper chamber and in the courtyard — go forth and learn from Elijah the prophet, remembered for good. And through the sin his body was disqualified from attaining ascent to a holy place; and when the possibility of separation from the body is removed, he lacks the attainment of eternal, longed-for sustenance. Accordingly, even though El did not command the man not to eat from the tree of life, the man on his own would refrain out of fear from the day he ate from the tree of knowledge — for otherwise there is no means to rectify the prohibition and the man is lost. But after he ate — it is true that if it had been in his mind that his eating was an eating that requires death for it, obviously HaShem would not have been concerned that he would eat from the tree of life; but his eating was inadvertent, as we have written above, and from now on he has no further cause for concern over the sin to guard himself from approaching the tree. <b>For</b> you must know that the principal reason for the King's commandment concerning the tree of knowledge is that the awareness of the aspect of evil not be grasped in the mind of man, and that his mind be absolute in the aspect of good, without any sensation of the aspect of evil and its existence in the world. And this is what it says (Ecclesiastes 7:29) ״עָשָׂה״ etc. ״אֶת הָאָדָם יָשָׁר״ — meaning, in one knowledge, good, with no imagination of evil depicted in his mind. And once evil became recognized in his knowledge and mind — and this is your sign: ״וַיֵּדְעוּ כִּי עֵירֻמִּים וַיִּתְבּוֹשָׁשׁוּ״ — there is no place for this commandment in the world. And the man would reckon that this commandment had voided itself, and from now on he would not be concerned to stretch out his hand and live; and HaShem said that he is mistaken in his mind, and he must die even for his error which he erred for the reason we have written above — for his body was disqualified from ascent to Elohim together with the soul." "<b>And according to</b> this approach we will understand one baffling statement (Genesis Rabbah 21:6), and this is their language: ״וְעַתָּה פֶּן יִשְׁלַח יָדוֹ״ — Rabbi Abba bar Kahana said: this teaches that the Holy One, blessed be He, opened for him a door of repentance, as it is written ״וְעַתָּה״ — ״וְעַתָּה״ (and now) means nothing other than repentance, as it is stated (Deuteronomy 10:12): ״וְעַתָּה יִשְׂרָאֵל מָה״ etc., and He said ״פֶּן״ — ״פֶּן״ means nothing other than a negative (לָאו). The Holy One, blessed be He, said: he will send his hand and eat, and if he eats he will live forever — end of their language. Their words, may their memory be for blessing, are extremely baffling: how can it be justified to say that HaShem told man to repent and rebel with so great a rebellion as to say ״no״? Further, why was HaShem not concerned that he would eat unless he said ״no״? And it would seem plausible to say that if he had done repentance, HaShem would not have insisted on his dying forever — but this is not so, for he had already incurred the death penalty on the day he ate from the Tree of Knowledge." "<b>And according to</b> our approach, the words of our Sages, may their memory be for blessing, will be understood correctly: for HaShem wished to inform man that he had sinned in his eating, and through the medium of his recognition of this, he would on his own abstain from eating of the Tree of Life, as we wrote above, for this is his repair. And therefore He told him to do repentance, and the implication of repentance would indicate that he had transgressed. But man answered ״no,״ meaning that he had not sinned such that he needed to return, and he puts forward an audible claim that he did not know, and is only an unwitting transgressor, and does not need to do repentance. And since he made this claim, HaShem said: from now on he will send his hand and eat, for there is nothing deterring him according to his own reasoning. Therefore HaShem banished him, for his sentence was sealed to die for his eating, for the reason we have written. And afterward he repented and entered the waters of the Gihon for one hundred and thirty years to atone for his sin (Pirkei de-Rabbi Eliezer 20)." "<b>Now behold,</b> even though HaShem said to him ״בְּיוֹם אֲכָלְךָ מִמֶּנּוּ״ etc., the words of the Holy One, blessed be He, are in two senses: there is in the implication of ״day״ — man's own day, and there is in the implication of the day of the Holy One, blessed be He, which is a thousand years (Genesis Rabbah, chapter 19). And the intention is according to the measure of the sin and according to his awareness of the matter: if he is deemed guilty of transgressing the word of HaShem brazenly — his own day will be fulfilled in him; and if he commits an unwitting error of such a kind and reveals his mind that he had no desire for the matter — the intention will be the day of the Holy One, blessed be He, a thousand years, as indeed it was."], :book :genesis, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 3, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:39:36.246309131Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}